00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:31.160
They are talking
about dark launches

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:32.540
and what it does for me.

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:36.080
So basically, if you are,
say, reimplementing a service

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:39.050
or adding new functionality
into a service that

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:41.090
could potentially
break old things,

00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:42.639
you can run it in
the background,

00:01:42.639 --> 00:01:44.180
and they talk about
how you can proxy

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:47.210
the traffic between the old
service and the new service.

00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:49.610
That new service-- the
customer never sees it.

00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:51.110
The end user never
sees it, and then

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:53.270
you diff the data between
and see what's changed

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:54.290
and whether it's
actually doing what

00:01:54.290 --> 00:01:56.180
it's supposed to be doing
with real information.

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:57.096
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:01:57.096 --> 00:01:59.570
I actually did this in
my previous company.

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:02.330
Before joining Google, I was
working in a company, Amadeus,

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:07.250
and we migrated out of an
IBM mainframe called TPF.

00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:11.840
And we rebuilt the whole thing
on there like on Linux and with

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:15.150
C++ to make sure that these
actually were the best way was

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:15.650
doing this.

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:19.160
You have a proxy, you get all
of the traffic from production,

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:22.309
you send that to both
the old and new services,

00:02:22.309 --> 00:02:24.350
and then every single time
you get the responses.

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:25.058
You compare them.

00:02:25.058 --> 00:02:28.070
You always send the one that you
trust, which is the old system.

00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:30.680
But every single time there's
a difference, you log it

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:32.270
and you find an issue.

00:02:32.270 --> 00:02:34.730
And with that, by the time you
don't have any more issues,

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:37.340
it means that you're pretty
much ready to migrate.

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:38.110
