00:05:57.880 --> 00:05:58.796
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:05:58.796 --> 00:06:01.820
So I guess that you should be
able to answer this question.

00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:03.610
What is Heroic Labs?

00:06:03.610 --> 00:06:05.090
What do they do?

00:06:05.090 --> 00:06:07.580
MO FIROUZ: So we build
server infrastructure

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:09.290
for games and apps.

00:06:09.290 --> 00:06:12.706
We provide an open source
server called Nakama.

00:06:12.706 --> 00:06:13.830
That's on GitHub right now.

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:15.140
You can go and download it.

00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:19.640
And what it does, it takes
care of all the infrastructure

00:06:19.640 --> 00:06:22.590
needs, all the back-end
needs of a game or an app.

00:06:22.590 --> 00:06:25.340
We have social APIs for games.

00:06:25.340 --> 00:06:29.750
We have social APIs for chat,
for real time, multiplayer,

00:06:29.750 --> 00:06:33.680
leaderboards, data storage, and
basically all sorts of building

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:38.440
blocks that games and apps
need to build to service

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:40.490
a live game or a live app.

00:06:40.490 --> 00:06:44.340
ALIM JAFFER: So in short, our
primary product is Nakama.

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:47.510
It's an open source
distributed game server.

00:06:47.510 --> 00:06:51.500
It's designed to power an entire
back-end for games and apps.

00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:54.140
One of the big changes for us
was going the open source route

00:06:54.140 --> 00:06:56.540
with Nakama, and we've had a
phenomenal reception to it.

00:06:56.540 --> 00:06:58.790
And we've designed it
to be real time first.

00:06:58.790 --> 00:07:01.248
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I want to make
sure I understand correctly

00:07:01.248 --> 00:07:02.600
what Nakama is.

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:04.580
So is it more like a framework?

00:07:04.580 --> 00:07:07.310
Is it a library can be
used any language with it?

00:07:07.310 --> 00:07:08.990
What does it look
like to develop

00:07:08.990 --> 00:07:10.080
a game on top of Nakama?

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:10.830
ALIM JAFFER: Sure.

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:14.660
So it is your entire back-end,
from everything from your user

00:07:14.660 --> 00:07:18.110
accounts to your data storage
to real-time multiplayer

00:07:18.110 --> 00:07:19.280
to matchmaking.

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:20.330
We handle all of that.

00:07:20.330 --> 00:07:23.000
So you could build, say,
a Clash Royale-style game

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:25.790
off of Nakama and not need
any third party services,

00:07:25.790 --> 00:07:27.940
except for things like
analytics and anything

00:07:27.940 --> 00:07:30.050
you wanted to do on
the data side there.

00:07:30.050 --> 00:07:32.810
It's written in Google's
Go programming language,

00:07:32.810 --> 00:07:35.870
and we have an embedded
scripting engine written, Lua,

00:07:35.870 --> 00:07:38.690
so that you can add custom
scripts to it to customize

00:07:38.690 --> 00:07:40.190
to suit more your
specific gameplay.

00:07:40.190 --> 00:07:41.106
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:07:41.106 --> 00:07:44.060
So does that mean that in order
to write a video game on top

00:07:44.060 --> 00:07:46.370
of Nakama I have to use Go?

00:07:46.370 --> 00:07:47.377
Or how does that work?

00:07:47.377 --> 00:07:48.710
ALIM JAFFER: No, absolutely not.

00:07:48.710 --> 00:07:51.380
So we have a first-class
client [INAUDIBLE]

00:07:51.380 --> 00:07:53.530
for Unity, for Unreal Engine.

00:07:53.530 --> 00:07:55.910
We're going to be
releasing a JavaScript

00:07:55.910 --> 00:07:57.800
one in the next
few weeks, as we've

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:00.680
had a lot of demand for HTML5
games and also just apps

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:01.490
in general.

00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:04.220
Because what you power for games
is really a superset for apps.

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:05.450
So anything that
can power a game

00:08:05.450 --> 00:08:06.866
can certainly power
an app, and we

00:08:06.866 --> 00:08:09.230
plan on expanding
this to kind of become

00:08:09.230 --> 00:08:12.410
a base framework for games
and apps moving forward.

00:08:12.410 --> 00:08:13.160
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:15.987
So now I really want to get into
the GCP side of things as well,

00:08:15.987 --> 00:08:17.570
but what I really
want to know as well

00:08:17.570 --> 00:08:21.320
is you said this is an open
source product that you provide

00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:22.160
a source for.

00:08:22.160 --> 00:08:23.380
Why is it open source?

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:25.880
Can you talk a little bit about
that decision making process

00:08:25.880 --> 00:08:27.861
and why you ended up
building it that way?

00:08:27.861 --> 00:08:29.110
ALIM JAFFER: Yeah, absolutely.

00:08:29.110 --> 00:08:31.610
It was quite the contentious
issue internally for a while.

00:08:31.610 --> 00:08:33.830
It was this strong debate
of do you go open source

00:08:33.830 --> 00:08:36.360
and give away essentially
your secret sauce

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:38.360
or do you keep offering
a closed source solution

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:40.460
as so many people have?

00:08:40.460 --> 00:08:41.690
One of the big pushes--

00:08:41.690 --> 00:08:43.159
we were already
working towards it,

00:08:43.159 --> 00:08:46.760
but when Parse announced their
shut down, it did a lot of harm

00:08:46.760 --> 00:08:48.860
to the industry, both on
the games and apps side,

00:08:48.860 --> 00:08:50.870
where people went,
oh, it turns out

00:08:50.870 --> 00:08:52.160
we can't trust these things.

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:55.280
And there's already a large
not-invented-here syndrome

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:58.310
in the industry where people
want to build their own tecg.

00:08:58.310 --> 00:08:59.890
So by going open
source, we wanted

00:08:59.890 --> 00:09:02.330
to offer people both
the comfort of knowing

00:09:02.330 --> 00:09:04.010
they can own the
technology, but also

00:09:04.010 --> 00:09:07.370
the transparency behind it,
where they get to work with it,

00:09:07.370 --> 00:09:09.830
do what they want with
it, fork it, modify,

00:09:09.830 --> 00:09:11.390
have it suit their needs.

00:09:11.390 --> 00:09:13.430
But the big thing was
to offer it to everyone

00:09:13.430 --> 00:09:15.650
and go, look, it's not
going to disappear.

00:09:15.650 --> 00:09:18.440
You can play a part of this and
we can build something bigger

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:19.220
moving forward.

00:09:19.220 --> 00:09:21.140
It gives you the
ownership and control,

00:09:21.140 --> 00:09:23.474
but simultaneously you get
something that's productized.

00:09:23.474 --> 00:09:25.556
Because we're still working
on it every single day

00:09:25.556 --> 00:09:26.990
and constantly
adding improvements

00:09:26.990 --> 00:09:28.940
to it, along with the
community members who

00:09:28.940 --> 00:09:29.960
are also contributing.

00:09:29.960 --> 00:09:33.020
So it's a bit about growing
that community out and also

00:09:33.020 --> 00:09:34.820
getting adoption out
there, and open source

00:09:34.820 --> 00:09:36.570
is by far the best
route forward for that.

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:37.486
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:09:37.486 --> 00:09:39.020
I was checking
right now the fact

00:09:39.020 --> 00:09:41.900
that you actually have
all of your code at GitHub

00:09:41.900 --> 00:09:43.400
under Heroic Labs.

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:45.170
And you even have
all the planning

00:09:45.170 --> 00:09:47.777
on what you're working on.

00:09:47.777 --> 00:09:49.610
ALIM JAFFER: Yeah, we're
real firm believers

00:09:49.610 --> 00:09:52.220
in the open source philosophy
and that our roadmap needs

00:09:52.220 --> 00:09:54.680
to be public, and
we want influence.

00:09:54.680 --> 00:09:57.600
Basically, what we work on
next is based on our customers

00:09:57.600 --> 00:09:58.970
and based on our community.

00:09:58.970 --> 00:10:00.980
So when they say, oh,
hey, we want matchmaking,

00:10:00.980 --> 00:10:02.420
we're starting to get
our games out there

00:10:02.420 --> 00:10:04.080
and we need a better
way to matchmake,

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:06.120
that became our next priority.

00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:08.040
And it's something
we're firm believers in,

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:10.480
that we don't build
games ourselves.

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:12.060
It's not our desire to.

00:10:12.060 --> 00:10:14.760
But those who are building
games can help us work together

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:17.220
on here's what we need
and here's why we need it,

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:19.470
and we can make sure it
works at a scalable level.

00:10:19.470 --> 00:10:21.261
MARK MANDEL: Now, I
know as well-- and, Mo,

00:10:21.261 --> 00:10:23.849
maybe this is a
question for you--

00:10:23.849 --> 00:10:25.890
I know on the website it
talks about CockroachDB,

00:10:25.890 --> 00:10:29.580
but you build your platform
on top of other open source

00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:30.370
products as well.

00:10:30.370 --> 00:10:31.560
Can you talk a bit
about what those are

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:33.090
and why you made
those decisions?

00:10:33.090 --> 00:10:35.220
MO FIROUZ: Yes, so
Cockroach being obviously

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:38.250
the biggest one, because that's
the only database that Nakama

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:40.680
needs to basically run.

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:42.690
Nakama's and our philosophy is--

00:10:42.690 --> 00:10:44.450
if you think about
the cap theorem,

00:10:44.450 --> 00:10:46.410
Nakama is the high
availability sort of stuff,

00:10:46.410 --> 00:10:49.140
and Cockroach is the
consistency sort of stuff.

00:10:49.140 --> 00:10:51.852
And we sort of give
the strong constistency

00:10:51.852 --> 00:10:53.310
Cockroach will take
care of for us,

00:10:53.310 --> 00:10:56.640
while we take care of being
highly available to the games

00:10:56.640 --> 00:10:58.290
for various use cases.

00:10:58.290 --> 00:11:02.550
We use various technologies
on top of Nakama

00:11:02.550 --> 00:11:04.230
and to build Nakama on.

00:11:04.230 --> 00:11:07.230
So I'd like to say Kubernetes,
for instance, is one of them.

00:11:07.230 --> 00:11:10.800
Ansible is another one to drive
various parts of the deployment

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:11.850
process.

00:11:11.850 --> 00:11:15.270
We use various Go
libraries, like Zap for log

00:11:15.270 --> 00:11:19.220
in within Go or protocol
buffers, for instance.

00:11:19.220 --> 00:11:22.530
We use Erlang and [? LXE ?]
in part of our platform,

00:11:22.530 --> 00:11:25.980
and it's a combination of all
of those open source projects

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:28.844
that have sort of led to Nakama
being what it is right now.

00:11:28.844 --> 00:11:29.760
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:31.843
I really like that you're
using so many languages.

00:11:31.843 --> 00:11:33.540
Sounds very exciting.

00:11:33.540 --> 00:11:35.189
Are you hiring?

00:11:35.189 --> 00:11:36.730
MO FIROUZ: We are
always hiring, yes.

00:11:36.730 --> 00:11:38.550
We can talk about that
after the podcast.

00:11:38.550 --> 00:11:40.405
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:11:40.405 --> 00:11:40.905
Good.

00:11:40.905 --> 00:11:41.880
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:43.464
MARK MANDEL: Francesc,
don't leave me.

00:11:43.464 --> 00:11:45.421
ALIM JAFFER: No free
lunch like Google, though.

00:11:45.421 --> 00:11:45.921
I'm sorry.

00:11:45.921 --> 00:11:47.379
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Oh, yeah, then no.

00:11:47.379 --> 00:11:49.170
So now that we've
talked a little bit

00:11:49.170 --> 00:11:51.630
about all the open
source side of things,

00:11:51.630 --> 00:11:54.912
you mentioned that you run
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:11:54.912 --> 00:11:57.120
Could you tell us a little
bit how does it look like?

00:11:57.120 --> 00:11:59.220
What products are
you using exactly?

00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:02.130
MO FIROUZ: So I guess
the main crust of it

00:12:02.130 --> 00:12:04.510
all is the Container Engine.

00:12:04.510 --> 00:12:07.320
We use Container Engine
to power Kubernetes,

00:12:07.320 --> 00:12:10.320
and obviously that uses
Compute Engine Instances.

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:11.990
And part of the
Container Engine is

00:12:11.990 --> 00:12:13.480
used to contain the registry.

00:12:13.480 --> 00:12:17.370
So we upload our images
into the Container Registry

00:12:17.370 --> 00:12:19.470
and get Docker
and get Kubernetes

00:12:19.470 --> 00:12:22.960
to run within the
Container Engine system.

00:12:22.960 --> 00:12:25.710
And part of that comes while
logging and monitoring that.

00:12:25.710 --> 00:12:29.206
We use Stackdriver for, pretty
much exclusively at this point,

00:12:29.206 --> 00:12:32.280
the sort of cool, nice
storage for storing

00:12:32.280 --> 00:12:35.390
various snapshots of
Cockroach's database

00:12:35.390 --> 00:12:38.970
for restore and backup purposes.

00:12:38.970 --> 00:12:43.740
And Log Balancer, both
sort of the L7 and L4

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:48.107
for load balances, for various
web socket and HTTP traffic.

00:12:48.107 --> 00:12:49.440
MARK MANDEL: Sounds really cool.

00:12:49.440 --> 00:12:53.419
How are you deploying your
Nakama onto GKE or Kubernetes?

00:12:53.419 --> 00:12:54.960
Are you doing anything
special there,

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:56.480
or is it just
straight deployments?

00:12:56.480 --> 00:12:57.640
Or can you talk about that?

00:12:57.640 --> 00:12:59.265
MO FIROUZ: So remember
when I mentioned

00:12:59.265 --> 00:13:02.970
that we run a few of
the path from code

00:13:02.970 --> 00:13:05.070
in [? LXE ?] on Erlang?

00:13:05.070 --> 00:13:08.940
So that niche of
the platform is what

00:13:08.940 --> 00:13:12.120
drives how we run
our manage cloud

00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:15.930
and how we deploy Nakama
into Google Cloud Platform.

00:13:15.930 --> 00:13:18.210
So obviously we
have a build box.

00:13:18.210 --> 00:13:21.450
That sort of builds the Nakama
images, the Docker images,

00:13:21.450 --> 00:13:23.710
pushes them into
Container Registry.

00:13:23.710 --> 00:13:26.070
Then we tell our
little app that we've

00:13:26.070 --> 00:13:28.230
created that lives
within Container Engine

00:13:28.230 --> 00:13:33.720
to talk to Kubernetes API to
create new server instances

00:13:33.720 --> 00:13:37.170
and run [INAUDIBLE] version
of Nakama and Cockroach

00:13:37.170 --> 00:13:39.690
we need to do, given the
configuration that we

00:13:39.690 --> 00:13:42.510
have for Kubernetes within
the Kubernetes [INAUDIBLE]

00:13:42.510 --> 00:13:43.480
effectively.

00:13:43.480 --> 00:13:48.670
That little Erlang and [? LXE ?]
app that we've written--

00:13:48.670 --> 00:13:50.730
we call it the manage
cloud servers--

00:13:50.730 --> 00:13:53.870
the actual app runs
Kubernetes and actually talks

00:13:53.870 --> 00:13:58.310
to the Kubernetes API, so we can
have a fully automated managed

00:13:58.310 --> 00:13:59.100
cloud system.

00:13:59.100 --> 00:14:01.740
MARK MANDEL: So does that mean
you wrote a client in Erlang

00:14:01.740 --> 00:14:03.650
for Cloud and Kubernetes?

00:14:03.650 --> 00:14:04.801
MO FIROUZ: Yes, we did.

00:14:04.801 --> 00:14:05.300
Yes.

00:14:05.300 --> 00:14:07.455
It wasn't fun, but yes, we did.

00:14:07.455 --> 00:14:09.330
MARK MANDEL: Are you
going to open source it?

00:14:09.330 --> 00:14:11.460
MO FIROUZ: I think it
needs a bit more cleanup

00:14:11.460 --> 00:14:13.410
in some places,
but we've already

00:14:13.410 --> 00:14:17.070
talked internally about open
sourcing part of the stock.

00:14:17.070 --> 00:14:19.560
Because one of the
reasons we actually

00:14:19.560 --> 00:14:23.762
went down to open source for
Nakama is what we've discovered

00:14:23.762 --> 00:14:26.220
and what we really like about
the gaming space is that it's

00:14:26.220 --> 00:14:29.040
sort of a tightly knit
industry, where everyone sort of

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:30.500
helps each other out.

00:14:30.500 --> 00:14:32.880
And we want to give back
something to the game industry

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:34.980
and to the ecosystem.

00:14:34.980 --> 00:14:38.130
And taking that sort
of idea, we want

00:14:38.130 --> 00:14:41.520
to do the same thing
around certain libraries

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:44.740
or certain kinds, for instance--
in this case, Kubernetes'

00:14:44.740 --> 00:14:46.650
Erlang client or
[? LXE ?] client

00:14:46.650 --> 00:14:48.150
and give that back
to the community.

00:14:48.150 --> 00:14:50.521
Because we appreciate
a lot and obviously

00:14:50.521 --> 00:14:52.020
have taken a lot
from the committee,

00:14:52.020 --> 00:14:54.310
and I think it's time for
us to give something back.

00:14:54.310 --> 00:14:56.025
So we will do that
at some point, yes.

00:14:56.025 --> 00:14:56.280
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:14:56.280 --> 00:14:58.150
At some point you
were talking about how

00:14:58.150 --> 00:15:02.070
you are able to scale while
keeping strong consistency,

00:15:02.070 --> 00:15:05.270
and I'm curious about
how you do that exactly.

00:15:05.270 --> 00:15:09.230
You mentioned that
you're using CockroachDB.

00:15:09.230 --> 00:15:13.580
Are those CockroachDB instances
running on top of Kubernetes

00:15:13.580 --> 00:15:14.510
on GKE?

00:15:14.510 --> 00:15:16.029
Do you run it separately?

00:15:16.029 --> 00:15:16.820
How does that work?

00:15:16.820 --> 00:15:22.100
MO FIROUZ: So Cockroach does
run on GKE and runs on GCP.

00:15:22.100 --> 00:15:24.890
Cockroach is basically
distributed SQL engine that's

00:15:24.890 --> 00:15:29.000
highly available, and they claim
that it survives disasters.

00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:30.980
And what we've seen
so far is that it

00:15:30.980 --> 00:15:34.670
does survive disasters-- not
that we've gone through many

00:15:34.670 --> 00:15:36.600
of the cases so far yet.

00:15:36.600 --> 00:15:38.690
What we can do
with CockroachDB is

00:15:38.690 --> 00:15:40.890
we can cluster them together.

00:15:40.890 --> 00:15:44.130
And we can start with,
say, one node of Cockroach.

00:15:44.130 --> 00:15:45.060
We can use that.

00:15:45.060 --> 00:15:45.870
It fills up.

00:15:45.870 --> 00:15:47.709
We put two or three
or four of the nodes

00:15:47.709 --> 00:15:49.250
together to get it
to form a cluster,

00:15:49.250 --> 00:15:51.920
and when it's true in the
cluster, it will sort of load

00:15:51.920 --> 00:15:54.560
balance the data on
the disk and copies

00:15:54.560 --> 00:15:56.840
it across to various
nodes while it's

00:15:56.840 --> 00:15:58.500
trying to join the cluster.

00:15:58.500 --> 00:16:00.530
So we will always have
the high availability

00:16:00.530 --> 00:16:03.310
and strongly consistent data.

00:16:03.310 --> 00:16:06.890
It's not like, say, for
instance, Cassandra database

00:16:06.890 --> 00:16:12.260
where your client needs to talk
with a specific set of nodes

00:16:12.260 --> 00:16:14.240
in the cluster,
not all the nodes.

00:16:14.240 --> 00:16:16.820
What happens in Cockroach is
you talk to one cluster node,

00:16:16.820 --> 00:16:18.290
and it sort of
replicates the data

00:16:18.290 --> 00:16:22.294
in the background transparently
for you across the other nodes.

00:16:22.294 --> 00:16:23.210
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:16:23.210 --> 00:16:28.550
And so I know that CockroachDB
is based somehow on Spanner

00:16:28.550 --> 00:16:31.550
and that one of the
powers of Spanner

00:16:31.550 --> 00:16:33.560
is the fact that it
uses atomic clocks.

00:16:33.560 --> 00:16:37.850
Could you talk a little bit
about is CockroachDB also

00:16:37.850 --> 00:16:42.830
using the NTP service that
Google Cloud Platform provides?

00:16:42.830 --> 00:16:43.880
How does it scale?

00:16:43.880 --> 00:16:46.490
Do you know this,
or is it just magic?

00:16:46.490 --> 00:16:48.410
MO FIROUZ: So it
is partly magic.

00:16:48.410 --> 00:16:49.960
When you're running
CockroachDB, you

00:16:49.960 --> 00:16:53.690
don't really get exposed
to that level of detail.

00:16:53.690 --> 00:16:57.020
We certainly haven't told it
to use any sort of NTP service

00:16:57.020 --> 00:16:58.400
within GCP.

00:16:58.400 --> 00:17:00.980
It does come with
NTP services inside,

00:17:00.980 --> 00:17:03.950
and it does seem to get
between different nodes.

00:17:03.950 --> 00:17:05.569
It's sort of very
much hands-off.

00:17:05.569 --> 00:17:07.730
You basically tell
it, so here is

00:17:07.730 --> 00:17:12.130
where the Cockroach nodes lives
and go and connect to them.

00:17:12.130 --> 00:17:14.089
And it sort of
figures out which node

00:17:14.089 --> 00:17:16.440
came first, what is
sort of the master node

00:17:16.440 --> 00:17:19.430
where the data exists,
although the term "master"

00:17:19.430 --> 00:17:21.980
is a very specific term, a
sort of distributed system.

00:17:21.980 --> 00:17:23.470
And how to
replicate-- it sort of

00:17:23.470 --> 00:17:25.640
uses the [INAUDIBLE]
protocol to know

00:17:25.640 --> 00:17:29.180
exactly where the notes are
and what data to replicate

00:17:29.180 --> 00:17:30.560
and how to do it, rather.

00:17:30.560 --> 00:17:31.476
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:17:31.476 --> 00:17:34.485
So I'm going to stop asking
you questions on databases.

00:17:34.485 --> 00:17:36.320
It's really interesting.

00:17:36.320 --> 00:17:38.210
Maybe we should
also have an episode

00:17:38.210 --> 00:17:39.790
with CockroachDB or something.

00:17:39.790 --> 00:17:41.930
CockroachDB people, if
you're listening to this,

00:17:41.930 --> 00:17:44.240
please let us know if
you'd like to come.

00:17:44.240 --> 00:17:50.660
But you say that you run all of
your service basically on GKE.

00:17:50.660 --> 00:17:51.980
How do you deploy?

00:17:51.980 --> 00:17:53.570
Are you using Container Builder?

00:17:53.570 --> 00:17:55.937
Are you using
something homemade?

00:17:55.937 --> 00:17:57.020
MARK MANDEL: Like Jenkins.

00:17:57.020 --> 00:17:58.603
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Spinnaker or Jenkins.

00:17:58.603 --> 00:17:59.560
Or how does it work?

00:17:59.560 --> 00:18:03.860
MO FIROUZ: We do use a slightly
modified version of Jenkins,

00:18:03.860 --> 00:18:05.570
sort of as a build box.

00:18:05.570 --> 00:18:08.690
And to drive the build box
and to drive the deployment,

00:18:08.690 --> 00:18:09.770
we use Ansible.

00:18:09.770 --> 00:18:14.390
And to drive Ansible, we
have we have a bot in a Slack

00:18:14.390 --> 00:18:17.400
that we type commands on
to basically drive Ansible

00:18:17.400 --> 00:18:19.910
and tell it commands
to do through the bot.

00:18:19.910 --> 00:18:25.420
So if we are out in bars or in
pubs, as we have it in London,

00:18:25.420 --> 00:18:27.490
we can just basically
connect to the Ansible bot

00:18:27.490 --> 00:18:30.980
through the Slack app and just
tell it to redeploy something.

00:18:30.980 --> 00:18:33.100
That's sort of the
methodology around it.

00:18:33.100 --> 00:18:35.840
ALIM JAFFER: Yeah, I can't
remember what movie we went to.

00:18:35.840 --> 00:18:38.210
Was it the Jason
Bourne movie and Andre

00:18:38.210 --> 00:18:40.464
had to use the hero bot
and Slack for something?

00:18:40.464 --> 00:18:42.380
MO FIROUZ: I think it
was one of those things,

00:18:42.380 --> 00:18:45.890
because me and Chris
were quite into the movie

00:18:45.890 --> 00:18:49.340
and we didn't really care
at that point what happened.

00:18:49.340 --> 00:18:53.800
So Andre was the one on
call, so yes, Andre did that.

00:18:53.800 --> 00:18:55.840
ALIM JAFFER: It's an
incredibly useful tool.

00:18:55.840 --> 00:18:57.330
MARK MANDEL: And just for
clarification so that people

00:18:57.330 --> 00:18:58.955
know how they're
using the tool, you're

00:18:58.955 --> 00:19:02.240
using Ansible to push
changes like container images

00:19:02.240 --> 00:19:04.700
and stuff like that
into Kubernetes itself,

00:19:04.700 --> 00:19:06.780
or are you interacting
with, say, the cluster?

00:19:06.780 --> 00:19:08.810
Or is it across the whole thing?

00:19:08.810 --> 00:19:10.360
How is that working?

00:19:10.360 --> 00:19:11.860
MO FIROUZ: No, the
pushing the image

00:19:11.860 --> 00:19:15.930
are done by effectively
the build box that we have.

00:19:15.930 --> 00:19:19.980
So we push whatever code that
we need to push into GitHub,

00:19:19.980 --> 00:19:24.130
and then we have the hook that
runs a continuous integration

00:19:24.130 --> 00:19:28.700
hook that builds the
image on the build box.

00:19:28.700 --> 00:19:30.560
And then from
there, the image is

00:19:30.560 --> 00:19:32.810
ready inside Container Registry.

00:19:32.810 --> 00:19:36.295
And then we use Ansible
to say, OK, Google, sort

00:19:36.295 --> 00:19:39.980
of the manage cloud servers,
go and grab this image

00:19:39.980 --> 00:19:41.690
from the Container
Registry and tell

00:19:41.690 --> 00:19:44.540
Kubernetes to deploy that
into this server for this data

00:19:44.540 --> 00:19:45.112
space.

00:19:45.112 --> 00:19:45.820
MARK MANDEL: Wow.

00:19:45.820 --> 00:19:48.636
I actually didn't know
Ansible had Kubernetes stuff.

00:19:48.636 --> 00:19:49.760
That's actually super cool.

00:19:49.760 --> 00:19:51.718
MO FIROUZ: No, Ansible
doesn't have Kubernetes.

00:19:51.718 --> 00:19:54.560
Ansible talks with our
server, the manage cloud,

00:19:54.560 --> 00:19:55.860
the Erlang node.

00:19:55.860 --> 00:19:59.130
And then the Erlang node
drives Kubernetes and tells it,

00:19:59.130 --> 00:20:01.060
so here's the app
that I have, here

00:20:01.060 --> 00:20:03.180
is the namespace for
this particular person

00:20:03.180 --> 00:20:04.350
or particular customer.

00:20:04.350 --> 00:20:07.620
And the way we've designed
the Kubernetes namespace

00:20:07.620 --> 00:20:10.190
is one namespace per
customer, effectively,

00:20:10.190 --> 00:20:13.560
on that particular
Kubernetes clusters.

00:20:13.560 --> 00:20:15.990
And we work with
our manage cloud,

00:20:15.990 --> 00:20:18.660
saying, so this is the
customer's namespace

00:20:18.660 --> 00:20:20.480
and go and deploy
this particular image,

00:20:20.480 --> 00:20:24.450
say, Nakama 1.0, into
that person's namespace

00:20:24.450 --> 00:20:27.187
and update it all and
then obviously update

00:20:27.187 --> 00:20:29.520
the schema that needs to be
migrated and so on, so forth

00:20:29.520 --> 00:20:30.350
from there.

00:20:30.350 --> 00:20:31.225
MARK MANDEL: Got you.

00:20:31.225 --> 00:20:33.730
So you basically use the APIs
that Kubernetes has given you

00:20:33.730 --> 00:20:35.730
so that you can build
your own basically control

00:20:35.730 --> 00:20:38.515
plane to control exactly what's
going on inside your cluster.

00:20:38.515 --> 00:20:39.140
MO FIROUZ: Yes.

00:20:39.140 --> 00:20:41.940
And that Erlang node, that
manage cloud server node,

00:20:41.940 --> 00:20:47.520
also talks with the Google API
to set up various DNS records

00:20:47.520 --> 00:20:49.530
and set up various
firewall rules.

00:20:49.530 --> 00:20:52.440
Because there are certain
firewall rules, for instance,

00:20:52.440 --> 00:20:54.780
for web socket
connections that you

00:20:54.780 --> 00:20:58.800
can't set through the Kubernetes
ingress because they're just

00:20:58.800 --> 00:20:59.970
not exposed there.

00:20:59.970 --> 00:21:04.290
So we have to talk directly
with the Google Cloud Platform

00:21:04.290 --> 00:21:08.250
within sort of L7 load
balances, the configuration,

00:21:08.250 --> 00:21:10.770
to update and to say,
OK, set a timeout

00:21:10.770 --> 00:21:13.410
of this particular
connection to X minutes,

00:21:13.410 --> 00:21:16.200
for instance, so we can
have a live web circuit

00:21:16.200 --> 00:21:17.234
connection going.

00:21:17.234 --> 00:21:19.150
MARK MANDEL: So that was
actually interesting.

00:21:19.150 --> 00:21:20.550
That was an interesting
sort of lesson

00:21:20.550 --> 00:21:22.591
that you sort of seemed
to have learned about how

00:21:22.591 --> 00:21:24.120
to manage GCP with Kubernetes.

00:21:24.120 --> 00:21:26.340
Have there been any other
particularly good lessons

00:21:26.340 --> 00:21:27.270
that you've learned
along the way

00:21:27.270 --> 00:21:28.440
that possibly our
listeners might

00:21:28.440 --> 00:21:29.570
want to learn from as well?

00:21:29.570 --> 00:21:31.028
MO FIROUZ: From
the top of my mind,

00:21:31.028 --> 00:21:32.560
I can't think of anything else.

00:21:32.560 --> 00:21:35.070
There were a couple
of details that

00:21:35.070 --> 00:21:37.595
weren't mentioned in
documentation that we found out

00:21:37.595 --> 00:21:39.720
while we were implementing,
but there weren't many.

00:21:39.720 --> 00:21:42.570
There were maybe a
couple, but this was maybe

00:21:42.570 --> 00:21:43.790
four or five months ago.

00:21:43.790 --> 00:21:47.280
So I can't recall exactly
what those details were.

00:21:47.280 --> 00:21:50.790
But honestly, generally
speaking so far,

00:21:50.790 --> 00:21:53.520
I'm really, really
happy with Kubernetes,

00:21:53.520 --> 00:21:56.060
and I'm really happy with
Google Cloud Platform.

00:21:56.060 --> 00:21:57.490
I can see that hands down.

00:21:57.490 --> 00:22:00.480
It's been a breeze.

00:22:00.480 --> 00:22:01.230
ALIM JAFFER: Yeah.

00:22:01.230 --> 00:22:04.920
I mean, we've done deployments
on Azure, on AWS, GCP,

00:22:04.920 --> 00:22:08.010
and I will say we've been
the happiest with GCP

00:22:08.010 --> 00:22:11.130
both for network reliability and
also for the services offered.

00:22:11.130 --> 00:22:13.420
It's kind of best suited
for us at the moment.

00:22:13.420 --> 00:22:15.420
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
since you mentioned

00:22:15.420 --> 00:22:18.960
that you've done deployments
on a bunch of different clouds,

00:22:18.960 --> 00:22:20.740
I wonder, why the Cloud?

00:22:20.740 --> 00:22:23.250
What are the benefits for
you to be running those game

00:22:23.250 --> 00:22:24.342
servers on the Cloud?

00:22:24.342 --> 00:22:25.800
ALIM JAFFER: With
the Cloud itself,

00:22:25.800 --> 00:22:28.416
we know how the Cloud
came to prominence 10,

00:22:28.416 --> 00:22:30.290
15 years ago, where it
really started to grow

00:22:30.290 --> 00:22:32.550
and allowed all these other
tech companies to come up.

00:22:32.550 --> 00:22:34.990
You no longer need to spend
two grand to get a basic web

00:22:34.990 --> 00:22:35.970
site running.

00:22:35.970 --> 00:22:38.370
You could start building
some really cool tech.

00:22:38.370 --> 00:22:41.550
And now that I feel the Cloud
is more or less mature, yes,

00:22:41.550 --> 00:22:43.840
there are some outages;
yes, things happen.

00:22:43.840 --> 00:22:46.950
But the general
usage of the Cloud--

00:22:46.950 --> 00:22:48.450
it's incredibly immature.

00:22:48.450 --> 00:22:51.000
And now as people are
getting content with it,

00:22:51.000 --> 00:22:53.440
there seems to be a bit of
an arms race, in my opinion--

00:22:53.440 --> 00:22:55.470
and, Mo, feel free to
jump in at any point--

00:22:55.470 --> 00:22:58.150
between AWS, GCP,
Azure, and all of them

00:22:58.150 --> 00:23:00.890
where something we call the
verticalization of the Cloud,

00:23:00.890 --> 00:23:04.590
where now they're starting to
offer more bespoke services

00:23:04.590 --> 00:23:05.550
for each vertical.

00:23:05.550 --> 00:23:08.400
So for example, I
know games is growing,

00:23:08.400 --> 00:23:10.927
and each Cloud is trying to
go after it more and more

00:23:10.927 --> 00:23:12.510
because it's going
to continue to grow

00:23:12.510 --> 00:23:13.780
and be larger and larger.

00:23:13.780 --> 00:23:16.770
And as games go online more,
as there's more multiplayer,

00:23:16.770 --> 00:23:19.710
as consumers expect
games to do more,

00:23:19.710 --> 00:23:22.560
it's going to take
more resources to do,

00:23:22.560 --> 00:23:24.570
and it also takes a lot
of complexity to build.

00:23:24.570 --> 00:23:26.695
So we kind of view the
verticalization of the Cloud

00:23:26.695 --> 00:23:30.210
as each cloud starting to offer
more bespoke services to build

00:23:30.210 --> 00:23:31.860
on top of, but
that way they don't

00:23:31.860 --> 00:23:34.530
have to start from scratch just
from the virtualization layer.

00:23:34.530 --> 00:23:37.050
And that's kind of also where
we see Nakama ongoing where

00:23:37.050 --> 00:23:40.870
it becomes a vertical upon
clouds to build forward.

00:23:40.870 --> 00:23:41.662
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:23:41.662 --> 00:23:43.620
Yeah, I just realized
one thing as you said it.

00:23:43.620 --> 00:23:45.674
Where does that name
Nakama come from?

00:23:45.674 --> 00:23:46.590
ALIM JAFFER: Oh, sure.

00:23:46.590 --> 00:23:48.580
So it's a Japanese word.

00:23:48.580 --> 00:23:52.110
So one of the guys, Chris,
one of the founders,

00:23:52.110 --> 00:23:54.727
is huge into anime,
loves it to death.

00:23:54.727 --> 00:23:57.060
We're trying to get him out
to Tokyo as soon as possible

00:23:57.060 --> 00:23:58.950
so he can fulfill that dream.

00:23:58.950 --> 00:24:01.760
And it means friends who are--

00:24:01.760 --> 00:24:03.840
I believe it literally
translates as "friends

00:24:03.840 --> 00:24:06.220
who are as close as family."

00:24:06.220 --> 00:24:08.010
And so the whole
point of Nakama was

00:24:08.010 --> 00:24:10.770
we believe games are
getting incredibly social,

00:24:10.770 --> 00:24:12.840
and it's very much
a social server.

00:24:12.840 --> 00:24:15.180
We have a social graph
built innately into it

00:24:15.180 --> 00:24:18.660
so you can do queries, like,
show me friends of my friends

00:24:18.660 --> 00:24:20.430
who are not yet my friends.

00:24:20.430 --> 00:24:22.791
And Nakama seemed to
suit that pretty well.

00:24:22.791 --> 00:24:24.290
It was going to be
the project name,

00:24:24.290 --> 00:24:26.250
and then we just all fell
in love with it and said,

00:24:26.250 --> 00:24:27.500
it's perfect for what we need.

00:24:27.500 --> 00:24:30.421
So friends who are as close
as family, essentially.

00:24:30.421 --> 00:24:31.170
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:24:31.170 --> 00:24:32.700
Well, unfortunately,
we're running out of time

00:24:32.700 --> 00:24:33.741
