00:26:37.995 --> 00:26:39.580
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Thanks to Alim and Mo

00:26:39.580 --> 00:26:41.969
from Heroic Labs for taking
the time today to tell us

00:26:41.969 --> 00:26:44.510
a little bit more about all the
cool video games that you can

00:26:44.510 --> 00:26:49.530
build on top of GKE quite easily
with the open source framework.

00:26:49.530 --> 00:26:51.380
They're open source,
so check it out.

00:26:51.380 --> 00:26:54.930
We'll have a link to their
GitHub repos on the show notes.

00:26:54.930 --> 00:26:56.674
It's [INAUDIBLE]
worth having a look

00:26:56.674 --> 00:26:57.840
and maybe even contributing.

00:26:57.840 --> 00:26:59.000
It's open source, after all.

00:26:59.000 --> 00:26:59.890
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, definitely.

00:26:59.890 --> 00:27:01.180
Super cool stuff
they're building.

00:27:01.180 --> 00:27:02.846
And yeah, I'm going
to be excited to see

00:27:02.846 --> 00:27:04.210
what they build on top of GKE.

00:27:04.210 --> 00:27:05.626
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
I think it's time

00:27:05.626 --> 00:27:07.940
to go with our
question of the week.

00:27:07.940 --> 00:27:10.170
And the question of
the week came yesterday

00:27:10.170 --> 00:27:11.680
from our coworkers,
because we're

00:27:11.680 --> 00:27:14.470
on the same team, Gabe Wise.

00:27:14.470 --> 00:27:18.760
He's a developer advocate that
just joined recently doing

00:27:18.760 --> 00:27:20.860
internet of things at
Google Cloud Platform,

00:27:20.860 --> 00:27:23.650
and something he was
doing is he's actually

00:27:23.650 --> 00:27:24.670
logging the temperature.

00:27:24.670 --> 00:27:26.860
He has a little sensor that
turns the current temperature

00:27:26.860 --> 00:27:27.430
through--

00:27:27.430 --> 00:27:29.260
I don't know exactly
what, but ends up

00:27:29.260 --> 00:27:31.320
running Cloud Functions.

00:27:31.320 --> 00:27:33.550
And then he wants to
store that piece of data

00:27:33.550 --> 00:27:37.210
from Cloud Functions to a SQL
instance running on Cloud SQL.

00:27:37.210 --> 00:27:40.420
Now, the problem is that in
order to access Cloud SQL,

00:27:40.420 --> 00:27:42.550
there's a couple of
ways of doing this.

00:27:42.550 --> 00:27:45.640
One of the ways is
by using App Engine

00:27:45.640 --> 00:27:49.490
or by using the App
Engine service account.

00:27:49.490 --> 00:27:53.110
Now, that does not work
with Cloud Functions,

00:27:53.110 --> 00:27:54.370
because it's not App Engine.

00:27:54.370 --> 00:27:55.610
It's a different environment.

00:27:55.610 --> 00:27:57.520
The other option
is to say, well,

00:27:57.520 --> 00:28:00.728
I'm going to allow the
connection from this IP.

00:28:00.728 --> 00:28:02.686
MARK MANDEL: But Cloud
Functions is serverless.

00:28:02.686 --> 00:28:03.630
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's
the whole point, yeah.

00:28:03.630 --> 00:28:05.750
MARK MANDEL: Is
there even an IP?

00:28:05.750 --> 00:28:08.430
FRANCESC CAMPOY: What
is the IP of no server?

00:28:08.430 --> 00:28:09.200
Zero?

00:28:09.200 --> 00:28:09.940
I don't know.

00:28:09.940 --> 00:28:10.790
MARK MANDEL: Negative
one, I think.

00:28:10.790 --> 00:28:13.100
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So yeah,
you cannot do that either.

00:28:13.100 --> 00:28:15.160
So how do you do it?

00:28:15.160 --> 00:28:18.190
And basically, the
solution is pretty simple.

00:28:18.190 --> 00:28:19.780
You just use a proxy.

00:28:19.780 --> 00:28:21.430
So what you're going
to do is there's

00:28:21.430 --> 00:28:24.010
going to be a proxy
running with a specific IP

00:28:24.010 --> 00:28:25.820
that you can then
hit from there.

00:28:25.820 --> 00:28:27.550
And whenever you
connect to that proxy,

00:28:27.550 --> 00:28:30.030
the proxy will connect
to the SQL instance.

00:28:30.030 --> 00:28:32.500
And since that proxy
has a stable IP,

00:28:32.500 --> 00:28:35.244
you can whitelist the
IP of the proxy instead.

00:28:35.244 --> 00:28:36.160
So it's pretty simple.

00:28:36.160 --> 00:28:37.870
It gives all the
security and stuff.

00:28:37.870 --> 00:28:40.510
And the cool thing is that you
don't even need to write this

00:28:40.510 --> 00:28:43.090
yourself, because
there's an open source

00:28:43.090 --> 00:28:46.510
solution that is written here
at Google Cloud Platform.

00:28:46.510 --> 00:28:48.070
It's called Cloud SQL Proxy.

00:28:48.070 --> 00:28:51.520
So we'll have a link in the show
notes to both the repo, which

00:28:51.520 --> 00:28:53.645
is worth checking out
and also documentation.

00:28:53.645 --> 00:28:55.270
MARK MANDEL: And it's
also worth noting

00:28:55.270 --> 00:28:56.769
that we did a
previous episode where

00:28:56.769 --> 00:28:58.085
we talked about this as well.

00:28:58.085 --> 00:28:58.960
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yes.

00:28:58.960 --> 00:29:02.650
We have the episode 81 where
we talked with Francis Perry

00:29:02.650 --> 00:29:03.940
and we talked about this.

00:29:03.940 --> 00:29:05.440
So it's basically
the same solution,

00:29:05.440 --> 00:29:07.900
but that time it
was about, well, I

00:29:07.900 --> 00:29:10.430
have a manage instance group.

00:29:10.430 --> 00:29:13.390
So you could have used
there an IP range,

00:29:13.390 --> 00:29:15.325
because you could define that.

00:29:15.325 --> 00:29:18.850
But with Cloud Functions
you cannot do that anymore.

00:29:18.850 --> 00:29:21.880
So yeah, for Cloud Functions,
it's actually pretty easy.

00:29:21.880 --> 00:29:24.460
Gabe did this yesterday
and it worked.

00:29:24.460 --> 00:29:26.720
So check it out if that's
what you're trying to do.

00:29:26.720 --> 00:29:27.470
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:29:27.470 --> 00:29:30.160
I like this as well because it
doesn't matter if you're using,

00:29:30.160 --> 00:29:31.060
say, Cloud Functions.

00:29:31.060 --> 00:29:32.410
You could use it from
manage instance groups.

00:29:32.410 --> 00:29:33.910
You can use it for a
bunch of other things.

00:29:33.910 --> 00:29:35.260
FRANCESC CAMPOY: You can even
use it from a [INAUDIBLE]

00:29:35.260 --> 00:29:35.710
if you want to.

00:29:35.710 --> 00:29:36.751
MARK MANDEL: If you want.

00:29:36.751 --> 00:29:38.720
So Cloud Proxy gives
you a lot of flexibility

00:29:38.720 --> 00:29:40.730
by giving you a lot of security
at the same time, too, which

00:29:40.730 --> 00:29:42.040
is also really, really nice.

00:29:42.040 --> 00:29:42.550
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:29:42.550 --> 00:29:44.383
That's the important
part, because we tried.

00:29:44.383 --> 00:29:48.550
It's like, maybe you can
whitelist 0.0.0.0/32.

00:29:48.550 --> 00:29:49.990
MARK MANDEL: Which you can.

00:29:49.990 --> 00:29:50.917
I mean, you shouldn't.

00:29:50.917 --> 00:29:52.750
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Actually,
